---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE, SOCIALS } from '../consts';
import { withBase } from '../utils/url';

// Bump this whenever the content below changes.
const LAST_UPDATED = new Date('2026-11-14T00:00:00Z');
const updated = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-US', {
  year: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
}).format(LAST_UPDATED);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`Who runs ${SITE.title}, how its posts are researched and written with Claude, and how to report a mistake.`}
  path="/about"
  narrow
>
  <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
  <p class="page-sub">Last updated {updated}</p>

  <div class="prose about">
    <p>
      {SITE.title} is a technical blog about AI, the web and the systems underneath them. Most
      posts take one claim that is going around, such as a benchmark score, a security statistic or
      a decades-old formula, and check it by recomputing the numbers from public data. When the
      claim holds up, the post says so. When it does not, the post shows where it breaks.
    </p>

    <h2>Who runs it</h2>
    <p>
      The site is run by {SITE.author}, who chooses the topics, reviews every post and publishes
      it. You can find {SITE.author} on {SOCIALS.map((s, i) => (
        <>{i > 0 ? ' and ' : ''}<a href={s.href} rel="me noopener">{s.label}</a></>
      ))}.
    </p>
    {/* TODO(owner): add one or two sentences of your own background here, if you want them public. */}

    <h2>How the posts are written</h2>
    <p>
      The posts are researched and written by Claude, an AI model from Anthropic, in working
      sessions with the site owner. That is stated here so that nobody has to guess. What makes the
      process worth reading is what is required of it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every claim needs a source a reader can open: official documentation, release notes, source
        code, a paper, or a measurement taken for the post and described with its method.
      </li>
      <li>
        Where a post measures something, the script and its output are published next to it, in the{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/sehyunnoh/techblog/tree/main/public/files" rel="noopener">files
        folder of the repository</a>, so the numbers can be rerun.
      </li>
      <li>
        Limits are written down. Each post says what it did not check, and a result that came out
        differently from what was expected is reported as it came out.
      </li>
      <li>
        At most one post is published per day, and nothing is published just to fill a slot.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={withBase('/guides')}>Guides</a> are reference material in the same spirit:
      explainers and how-tos with their sources, revised in place when the subject changes.
    </p>

    <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
    <p>
      Corrections are welcome and I would like to hear about them. The fastest way is to open an
      issue on the <a href="https://github.com/sehyunnoh/techblog" rel="noopener">site's
      repository</a>, which is public. You can also reach me on {SOCIALS.map((s, i) => (
        <>{i > 0 ? ' or ' : ''}<a href={s.href} rel="noopener">{s.label}</a></>
      ))}. The full history of every change to a post is in the repository's commits.
    </p>

    <h2>What this site is not</h2>
    <p>
      It does not sell anything, run sponsored posts or take payment to mention a product. If that
      ever changes, this page will say so before it happens. How readers' data is handled is
      covered on the <a href={withBase('/privacy')}>Privacy</a> page.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-title {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 2rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.028em;
    font-weight: 680;
  }

  .page-sub {
    margin: 0.4rem 0 1.75rem;
    color: var(--fg-faint);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .about {
    max-width: none;
  }
</style>
